{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1668 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "953237205434b8417583a0cb53d4eb3cd1c9603ca1a8a9573d5dfe91ab3c22a4" :edb/db-before-t 1667 :edb/db-after-t 1668 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534980 50 #inst "2026-10-08T13:03:44.385Z" 13194139534980 true] [17592186171774 1100 "For the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 2x^2-y,\n$$\n\nplot the slope field at the points $(1,1),$ $(0,1),$ and $(1,2).$" 13194139534980 true] [17592186171774 1100 "For the differential equation\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 2x^2-y$,\n\nplot the slope field at the points $(1,1)$, $(0,1)$, and $(1,2)$." 13194139534980 false] [17592186171774 1145 "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(1,1)$, $(0,1)$, and $(1,2)$.\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left.y'\\right|_{(1,1)}&=2(1)^2-1=1,\\\\\n\\left.y'\\right|_{(0,1)}&=2(0)^2-1=-1,\\\\\n\\left.y'\\right|_{(1,2)}&=2(1)^2-2=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3352/Source/Images/2e6c81d3cc119edb8bc6c9a5813acfef.png)" 13194139534980 false] [17592186171774 1145 "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(1,1),$ $(0,1),$ and $(1,2).$\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,1)} & =2(1)^{2}-1=1 \\\\ {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,1)} & =2(0)^{2}-1=-1 \\\\ {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,2)} & =2(1)^{2}-2=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044888/assets/0c06b4acee1445fea91bcbb7bed5a837a1dd494c9638a4f8945f5ac8dc226c22.png)" 13194139534980 true] [17592186171775 1100 "Consider the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = -2xy$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- At every point in the second quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative\n\n- At every point in the third quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative\n\n- At every point in the fourth quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive" 13194139534980 true] [17592186171775 1100 "Consider the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = -2xy$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. At every point in the second quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative\n2. At every point in the third quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative\n3. At every point in the fourth quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive" 13194139534980 false] [17592186171775 1145 "First, we define\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = -2xy\n$$\n\nThen, our differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x} = f(x,y)\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = 0\n$$\n\nwhen $x=0$ or $y=0$. The lines $x=0$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into the usual four quadrants.\n\nIf we compute the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each quadrant (by considering some test points, for example), we get the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3352/Source/Images/f5f03b1c9ed7aee9818764ccc0c59265.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false. If $(x,y)$ is in the second quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n$\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,y)} =f(x,y) \\gt 0$.\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x, y)$ in the second quadrant.\n- Statement II is true. If $(x,y)$ is in the third quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n$\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,y)} = f(x,y) \\lt 0$.\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x, y)$ in the third quadrant.\n- Statement III is true. If $(x,y)$ is in the fourth quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n$\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,y)} = f(x,y) \\gt 0$.\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x, y)$ in the fourth quadrant.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139534980 false] [17592186171775 1145 "First, we define $f(x,y) = -2xy.$ Then, our differential equation is $\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x} = f(x,y).$ \n\nNotice that $f(x,y) = 0$ when $x=0$ or $y=0.$ The lines $x=0$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into the usual four quadrants.\n\nIf we compute the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each quadrant (by considering some test points, for example), we get the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044888/assets/2c218c7df2bf4e159b4bffaccb4296beab8e3f44ab82d56b3589050af92ccf8e.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false. If $(x,y)$ is in the second quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,y)} =f(x,y)  \\gt     0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x, y)$ in the second quadrant.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $(x,y)$ is in the third quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,y)} = f(x,y)  \\lt     0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x, y)$ in the third quadrant.\n\n- Statement III is true. If $(x,y)$ is in the fourth quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,y)} = f(x,y)  \\gt     0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x, y)$ in the fourth quadrant.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139534980 true] [17592186171776 1100 "Which of the following could be the slope field for the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = x+y$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3352/Source/Images/93bde689614608eb91d38a348bf71381.png)" 13194139534980 false] [17592186171776 1100 "Which of the following could be the slope field for the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = x+y?$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044888/assets/3bd19865d2a1bb6bbb40c57112f269b51a3740d01da2c80b81db4eb84c6766f1.png)" 13194139534980 true] [17592186171776 1145 "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = f(x,y)\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ and $y$. This means that the slope varies with both $x$ and $y$.\n\nOf the given slope fields, the only one that varies with both $x$ and $y$ is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3352/Source/Images/a5252d432d85bf6081707a2e6a174d66.png)\n\nTo verify the selected field specifically for $y'=x+y$, notice that its segments are horizontal on $y=-x$, positive above that line, and negative below it. For example, the slopes at $(1,0)$ and $(0,-1)$ are $1$ and $-1$, respectively. These checks agree with the selected diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3352/Source/Images/70f3eb2943cb191f8644c5f157ecc858.png)" 13194139534980 false] [17592186171776 1145 "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = f(x,y).\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ and $y.$ This means that the slope varies with both $x$ and $y.$\n\nOf the given slope fields, the only one that varies with both $x$ and $y$ is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044888/assets/543bb98ff948d1186e05d84db7992f9d5033ba4eaf47a9787b6b5664b88510a2.png)\n\n \n\nTo see this more clearly, notice that if we draw a vertical line and a horizontal line, the slopes vary if we move along either of the two lines.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044888/assets/433f0cf3835465e7a0cca195f8b0f0b280984200d84f49533219f08967adc497.png)" 13194139534980 true] [17592186171777 1100 "Consider the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=(x+1)(y+1)$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- At every point $(x,y)$ where $x \\gt -1$ and $y \\gt -1,$ the slope of the slope field is positive\n\n- At every point $(x,y)$ where $x \\lt -1$ and $y \\lt -1,$ the slope of the slope field is positive\n\n- At every point $(x,y)$ where $x \\gt -1$ and $y \\lt -1,$ the slope of the slope field is negative" 13194139534980 true] [17592186171777 1100 "Consider the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=(x+1)(y+1)$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. At every point $(x,y)$ where $x \\gt -1$ and $y \\gt -1$, the slope of the slope field is positive\n2. At every point $(x,y)$ where $x \\lt -1$ and $y \\lt -1$, the slope of the slope field is positive\n3. At every point $(x,y)$ where $x \\gt -1$ and $y \\lt -1$, the slope of the slope field is negative" 13194139534980 false] [17592186171777 1145 "First, we define\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = (x+1)(y+1)\n$$\n\nThen, our differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x} = f(x,y)\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = 0\n$$\n\nwhen $x = -1$ or $y = -1$. This divides the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3352/Source/Images/0f8a9ed747232a1b4e5c6ff0861af3cd.png)\n\nIf we compute the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each of those four regions (by considering some test points, for example), we get the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3352/Source/Images/f0f41e3b1b7a1732c9b95aac285ec4f4.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. If $x \\gt -1$ and $y \\gt -1$, then according to our diagram, we have\n$\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,y)} =f(x,y) \\gt 0$.\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x, y)$ in this region.\n- Statement II is true. If $x \\lt -1$ and $y \\lt -1$, then according to our diagram, we have\n$\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,y)} =f(x,y) \\gt 0$.\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x, y)$ in this region.\n- Statement III is true. If $x \\gt -1$ and $y \\lt -1$, then according to our diagram, we have\n$\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,y)} =f(x,y) \\lt 0$.\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x, y)$ in this region.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139534980 false] [17592186171777 1145 "First, we define $f(x,y) = (x+1)(y+1).$ Then, our differential equation is $\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x} = f(x,y).$ \n\nNotice that $f(x,y) = 0$ when $x = -1$ or $y = -1.$ This divides the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044888/assets/e7b398c7ff971e39b15980078232556d23866ab2f65a05b7d8df9a5f67b797dc.png)\n\nIf we compute the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each of those four regions (by considering some test points, for example), we get the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044888/assets/943ed7c12c71438d567da5ec901435bae1d3c7e576ba9b6c933d9fddc4e42b8f.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. If $x \\gt -1$ and $y \\gt -1,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,y)} =f(x,y)  \\gt     0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x, y)$ in this region.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $x \\lt -1$ and $y \\lt -1,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,y)} =f(x,y)  \\gt     0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x, y)$ in this region.\n\n- Statement III is true. If $x \\gt -1$ and $y \\lt -1,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,y)} =f(x,y)  \\lt     0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x, y)$ in this region.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139534980 true] [17592186171968 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324489 13194139534980 true] [17592186171968 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324531 13194139534980 true] [17592186171968 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324538 13194139534980 true] [17592186171968 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324545 13194139534980 true] [17592186171968 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324552 13194139534980 true] [17592186171969 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324496 13194139534980 true] [17592186171969 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324503 13194139534980 true] [17592186171969 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324559 13194139534980 true] [17592186171969 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324566 13194139534980 true] [17592186171969 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324573 13194139534980 true] [17592186171970 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324468 13194139534980 true] [17592186171970 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324475 13194139534980 true] [17592186171970 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324482 13194139534980 true] [17592186171970 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324587 13194139534980 true] [17592186171971 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324510 13194139534980 true] [17592186171971 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324517 13194139534980 true] [17592186171971 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324524 13194139534980 true] [17592186171971 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324580 13194139534980 true] [17592186172566 1100 "Which of the following could be the slope field for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=xy?$" 13194139534980 true] [17592186172566 1100 "Which of the following could be the slope field for the differential equation $\\frac{dy}{dx} = xy$?" 13194139534980 false] [17592186172566 1145 "For $y'=xy$, slopes are zero on both coordinate axes. They are positive when $x$ and $y$ have the same sign and negative when their signs differ. The selected field has horizontal segments on both axes, positive slopes in quadrants I and III, and negative slopes in II and IV; the magnitude grows with $|xy|$." 13194139534980 false] [17592186172566 1145 "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ and $y.$ This means that the slope varies with both $x$ and $y.$\n\nOf the given slope fields, the only one that varies with both $x$ and $y$ is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044888/assets/a324b98fc4ec7a38ce6276a357a649751b316c0e61ddb193ce5f34caed608e98.png)\n\n \n\nTo see this more clearly, notice that if we draw a vertical line and a horizontal line, the slopes vary if we move along either of the two lines.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044888/assets/9d60c65cda12bb6ac800906e7889a8ecc395974dff0379130d647810c1623869.png)" 13194139534980 true] [17592186172575 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534980 true] [17592186172575 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534980 false] [17592186172575 1100 "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(x+1)^{2}(y-2)$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- At every point $(x,y)$ where $x<-1$ and $y>2,$ the slope of the slope field is positive\n- At every point $(x,y)$ where $x<-1$ and $y<2,$ the slope of the slope field is negative\n- At every point $(x,y)$ where $x>-1$ and $y<2,$ the slope of the slope field is negative" 13194139534980 true] [17592186172575 1100 "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{dy}{dx} = (x + 1)^{2}(y - 2)$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. At every point $(x, y)$ where $x < - 1$ and $y > 2$, the slope of the slope field is positive\n2. At every point $(x, y)$ where $x < - 1$ and $y < 2$, the slope of the slope field is negative\n3. At every point $(x, y)$ where $x > - 1$ and $y < 2$, the slope of the slope field is negative" 13194139534980 false] [17592186172575 1145 "First, we define $f(x,y)=(x+1)^{2}(y-2).$ Then, our differential equation is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).$ \n\nNotice that $f(x,y)=0$ when $x=-1$ or $y=2.$ This divides the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044888/assets/1f4b1b2ca736fb83eff0a4b85a3f479bf99b3b337492d0a77abe739038ed141f.png)\n\nIf we compute the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each of those four regions (by considering some test points, for example), we get the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044888/assets/fdafadd1631a75c2998aaed5c6bad62a3ea1dcc0ac57d245a00d167e74aa3bb4.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. If $x<-1$ and $y>2,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in this region.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $x<-1$ and $y<2,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)<0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x,y)$ in this region.\n\n- Statement III is true. If $x>-1$ and $y<2,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)<0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x,y)$ in this region." 13194139534980 true] [17592186172575 1145 "In every listed region, $x\\ne-1$, so $(x+1)^2>0$. The slope therefore has the sign of $y-2$. It is positive above $y=2$ and negative below, on either side of $x=-1$. All three statements are true: select **I, II, and III**." 13194139534980 false] [17592186324468 1017 #edb/ref 17592186324469 13194139534980 true] [17592186324468 1089 "q-119369" 13194139534980 true] [17592186324468 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534980 true] [17592186324468 1100 "Which of the following could be the slope field for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y-{x}^{2}?$" 13194139534980 true] [17592186324468 1113 #uuid "0fdcac11-a7b2-5665-a341-f378b359ea29" 13194139534980 true] [17592186324468 1145 "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ and $y.$ This means that the slope varies with both $x$ and $y.$\n\nOf the given slope fields, the only one that varies with both $x$ and $y$ is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044888/assets/1bd04b981c412d771617ffaaa7cedf88b063ae49ab567cd27d665eb381a1b648.png)\n\n  \n\nTo see this more clearly, notice that if we draw a vertical line and a horizontal line, the slopes vary if we move along either of the two lines.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044888/assets/83f2ac528d0e4bdd7e486284a9f3d5692e0111c5cdc58dcc554e12dd0185f15f.png)" 13194139534980 true] [17592186324469 1000 #edb/ref 17592186324470 13194139534980 true] [17592186324469 1000 #edb/ref 17592186324471 13194139534980 true] [17592186324469 1000 #edb/ref 17592186324472 13194139534980 true] [17592186324469 1000 #edb/ref 17592186324473 13194139534980 true] [17592186324469 1000 #edb/ref 17592186324474 13194139534980 true] [17592186324469 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534980 true] [17592186324469 1041 "selection" 13194139534980 true] [17592186324469 1104 #edb/ref 17592186324471 13194139534980 true] [17592186324469 1110 #uuid "b1c6d7f6-9b65-5167-9aed-5668a3e7a8b0" 13194139534980 true] [17592186324470 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044888/assets/7470460ab6f2d7e0bfde9585c6447a75abbea498d9c0628c23d6b69bc9738c46.png" 13194139534980 true] [17592186324470 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534980 true] [17592186324470 1111 #uuid "ed78d2ea-f4b9-5e3c-b5af-be56dbec8314" 13194139534980 true] [17592186324471 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044888/assets/1bd04b981c412d771617ffaaa7cedf88b063ae49ab567cd27d665eb381a1b648.png" 13194139534980 true] [17592186324471 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534980 true] [17592186324471 1111 #uuid "1cb515b1-5173-5458-8be2-49be2435908d" 13194139534980 true] [17592186324472 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044888/assets/d8a84684bd0ea9b96e9e3901b4dfec5e5568a80ad44202161a76414ce0a4edfa.png" 13194139534980 true] [17592186324472 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534980 true] [17592186324472 1111 #uuid "a7872c33-3488-509d-a696-6fc905fe7388" 13194139534980 true] [17592186324473 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044888/assets/f5ee96c6ad4540cde69b78af9a13e4513a24f6c537f69c4bee5ec0d440da56c2.png" 13194139534980 true] [17592186324473 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534980 true] [17592186324473 1111 #uuid "bbc4a9a3-060b-5cdd-9489-ba14d8ec6d40" 13194139534980 true] [17592186324474 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/d